        "html": "<p>The exam is organised into four domains. <strong>Health and fitness assessment (33%)</strong>: screening, risk stratification, resting measures, body composition, and fitness testing. <strong>Exercise prescription and implementation (40%)</strong>: the biggest domain — FITT-VP, aerobic and resistance programming, flexibility and neuromotor training, and special populations. <strong>Exercise counselling and behaviour modification (20%)</strong>: motivation, stages of change, motivational interviewing, and goal-setting. <strong>Risk management and professional responsibilities (7%)</strong>: scope of practice, informed consent, emergency planning, records, and safety.</p><p>Two study implications follow. First, prescription is worth two-fifths of the exam, so the FITT-VP numbers — the 150 minutes a week, the 40–59% and 60–89% heart-rate reserve bands, the 60–70% of 1RM for novices, the 2–3 minute strength rests — are the highest-yield facts in this course. Second, the exam tests decisions, not recall: the questions describe a client and ask what you do next, so practise with scenarios, not flashcards alone.</p>"
